!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/validate-sidecar.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/34/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/34/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/sidecar-common.tn"
@doc:"""
  Formal shape of a validate-layer sidecar (tests/class2/validate/**/*-expected.tn) -- a data
  document read against a schema that has already loaded. See README.md's "The Class 2 layers" and
  RUNNER.md.

  This is the layer where [TSON-DATA] §8.1's `validation` category finally has vectors: it is
  "reserved for data checked against a successfully loaded schema", which is exactly what a subject
  here is. The governing schema is named by the sidecar's `schema` short name and spliced into the
  subject's header as a real `!!schema`; the subject names its own root type the way any
  self-describing document does, with a type-ref on its root value.
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    The neutral half of a diagnostic, and deliberately only that half: §8.1's category, plus the RFC
    6901 pointer into the *data* document naming the value that failed. Never a message (that means
    parsing prose), never a position (RUNNER.md rule 4), and never a schema pointer -- where in the
    schema a constraint is written is an implementation's choice of route, and two conforming
    processors legitimately name different ones.

    `path` is optional and omitted rather than written `""` where a vector does not turn on it. The
    root value's own pointer *is* `""`, so the two are not interchangeable: a vector that writes it
    is claiming the failure is at the root, and one that omits it is claiming nothing.

    No vector here turns on the one category README.md records as unsettled -- an atom rejecting a
    token's *format*, which §5.2 phrases as a parse error while §8.1's own `parser` description
    covers structural mismatches and the check happens well after the parser has accepted the
    document. What §8.1 does settle is the neighbouring case: "range violations by the numeric atoms"
    are `validation` explicitly, and that is what an atom vector here exercises.

    A runner asserts that at least one reported diagnostic matches. A document may legitimately
    report more than one -- §8.1 asks a processor to continue after an error -- and a vector naming
    one of them is not a claim that it is the only one.
    """
  validate_error => vector_error & {
    path: text?
  }

  @doc:"""
    `valid` carries nothing and is written `_`. What is normative is that the document conforms --
    the *value* a read produces is the reader layer's subject (`class1/reader/`), stated there once
    in a model that has nothing to do with schemas, and restating it here would make a Class 2
    vector fail whenever a tree model changed shape.
    """
  validate_sidecar => sidecar_common & spliced_header & {
    ( valid: void | error: validate_error )
  }
}
